('13b1b80b-559d-45f7-91a1-3dc31757dc83', 'assistant', 'Certainly! To achieve even more accurate results in your calculator, especially with floating point arithmetic (like `0.1 + 0.2`), you should:

- Use the `mathjs` library''s formatting and precision features.
- Ensure that results are rounded or formatted with significant digits, but not unnecessarily truncated.

Here is an improved version using `mathjs`, with enhanced accuracy and better floating point handling:

---

**Install mathjs if you haven''t already:**
```bash
npm install mathjs
```

---

**Updated Calculator Component:**

```jsx
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { evaluate, format } from "mathjs";

function Calculator() {
  const [input, setInput] = useState("");
  const [result, setResult] = useState("");

  // Handle button clicks
  const handleClick = (value) => {
    setInput((prev) => prev + value);
  };

  // Evaluate the expression using mathjs with fixed precision
  const handleEqual = () => {
    try {
      // Evaluate the input
      const evalResult = evaluate(input);
      // Format result to up to 12 significant digits
      const formattedResult = format(evalResult, { precision: 12 });
      setResult(formattedResult);
      setInput(formattedResult);
    } catch (error) {
      setResult("Error");
      setInput("");
    }
  };

  // Clear input and result
  const handleClear = () => {
    setInput("");
    setResult("");
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ width: "220px", margin: "50px auto", textAlign: "center", padding: "20px", border: "1px solid #ccc", borderRadius: "8px" }}>
      <h2>Calculator</h2>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={input}
        readOnly
        style={{ width: "200px", height: "40px", fontSize: "20px", marginBottom: "10px", textAlign: "right" }}
        placeholder="0"
      />
      <div style={{ minHeight: "24px", fontSize: "18px", color: "#333", marginBottom: "8px" }}>
        {result !== "" && <span>= {result}</span>}
      </div>
      <div>
        {["7", "8", "9", "/"].map((val) => (
          <button key={val} onClick={() => handleClick(val)} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>{val}</button>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div>
        {["4", "5", "6", "*"].map((val) => (
          <button key={val} onClick={() => handleClick(val)} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>{val}</button>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div>
        {["1", "2", "3", "-"].map((val) => (
          <button key={val} onClick={() => handleClick(val)} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>{val}</button>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div>
        <button onClick={handleClear} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>C</button>
        <button onClick={() => handleClick("0")} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>0</button>
        <button onClick={() => handleClick(".")} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>.</button>
        <button onClick={() => handleClick("+")} style={{ width: "45px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px" }}>+</button>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button onClick={handleEqual} style={{ width: "190px", height: "45px", margin: "2px", fontSize: "18px", backgroundColor: "#4caf50", color: "#fff" }}>=</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Calculator;
```

---

**How is this more accurate?**
- Uses `mathjs.evaluate` for computation (avoids JavaScript floating point errors).
- Uses `mathjs.format` to display up to 12 significant digits, so results like `0.1 + 0.2` will display `0.3` instead of `0.30000000000000004`.
- Displays the result separately and puts it into the input for further calculations if desired.

**Example:**  
Typing `0.1+0.2` and pressing `=` will show `= 0.3`.

