('6dedcdcb-b6ae-4f3c-bbe3-402dae379e71', 'assistant', '--- ### What is the "messages" table? The **messages** table stores individual messages that are part of a conversation between users and an AI assistant (or similar system). Each record in this table represents one message, which could be a user question, an assistant reply, or other roles. --- ### Key Concepts and Columns - **id** A unique identifier for each message. - **role** Indicates who sent the message, such as "user" or "assistant". - **content** The main text of the message as sent or received. - **content_full** Sometimes stores a more detailed or complete version of the message, if applicable. - **sequence** The order of the message within the conversation (which message came first, second, etc.). - **tokens_prompt** & **tokens_completion** Technical counts of how many language model tokens were used in the prompt and completion. Useful for tracking resource usage. - **tenant_id** Identifies the organization or group (tenant) the message belongs to, which is useful in multi-tenant systems. - **conversation_id** Links the message to a specific conversation, so multiple messages can be grouped together as part of the same chat. - **user_id** Identifies which user sent the message. - **model_id** Tracks which AI model generated or interacted with the message. - **created_at** & **updated_at** Timestamps for when the message was first created and last updated. - **is_interrupted** Indicates if the message or its generation was interrupted (for example, if the response was cut off). --- ### Summary In summary, the **messages** table is central to storing and organizing every exchange within a conversation between users and the system. It helps keep track of who said what, when, and in what context, supporting features like conversation history, analytics, and multi-tenant operations. The table’s columns provide both basic details (like message text and sender) and technical information (like token counts and related models) for comprehensive data tracking. Certainly! Here’s a clear explanation of the **messages** table and what it is used for: --- ### What is the "messages" table? The **messages** table stores individual messages that are part of a conversation between users and an AI assistant (or similar system). Each record in this table represents one message, which could be a user question, an assistant reply, or other roles. --- ### Key Concepts and Columns - **id** A unique identifier for each message. - **role** Indicates who sent the message, such as "user" or "assistant". - **content** The main text of the message as sent or received. - **content_full** Sometimes stores a more detailed or complete version of the message, if applicable. - **sequence** The order of the message within the conversation (which message came first, second, etc.). - **tokens_prompt** & **tokens_completion** Technical counts of how many language model tokens were used in the prompt and completion. Useful for tracking resource usage. - **tenant_id** Identifies the organization or group (tenant) the message belongs to, which is useful in multi-tenant systems. - **conversation_id** Links the message to a specific conversation, so multiple messages can be grouped together as part of the same chat. - **user_id** Identifies which user sent the message. - **model_id** Tracks which AI model generated or interacted with the message. - **created_at** & **updated_at** Timestamps for when the message was first created and last updated. - **is_interrupted** Indicates if the message or its generation was interrupted (for example, if the response was cut off). --- ### Summary In summary, the **messages** table is central to storing and organizing every exchange within a conversation between users and the system. It helps keep track of who said what, when, and in what context, supporting features like conversation history, analytics, and multi-tenant operations. The table’s columns provide both basic details (like message text and sender) and technical information (like token counts and related models) for comprehensive data tracking. If you want to run the SQL query, connect to a database first. ', NULL, 2, NULL, NULL, '366c8e29-d8cd-4414-bbbc-aa5e6aed75db', '85fd7149-5599-4598-8f4b-29157e965ef2', '4ea5c16d-561d-4d25-8d4c-867994ccf463', NULL, '2025-10-24 06:20:03.635000+00:00', '2025-10-24 06:20:03.635000+00:00', FALSE, NULL, NULL),
