('c83e319e-83b3-4373-8049-4e58c4cb7353', 'assistant', 'To list all users on a Linux system, you can use one of the following commands in the terminal:

**Option 1: View /etc/passwd file**
```bash
cat /etc/passwd
```
This will show all user accounts (system and regular users).

**Option 2: List only usernames**
```bash
cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd
```
This displays just the usernames.

**Option 3: For systems with `getent`**
```bash
getent passwd
```
This works even with networked user databases.

If you need users with login shells (not system users), try:
```bash
awk -F: ''$7 ~ /bash|sh/ { print $1 }'' /etc/passwd
