('1ffbb405-0a0c-4d4f-bbd5-881977320386', 'assistant', 'Certainly! Here’s an example of how you can fetch and display all active tenants with their name, slug, and status using ReactJS. This assumes you have an API endpoint (for example, `/api/tenants`) that returns a list of active tenants in JSON format.

**ReactJS Example (Functional Component):**

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from ''react'';

function ActiveTenants() {
  const [tenants, setTenants] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetch active tenants from the API
    fetch(''/api/tenants?status=active'')
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((data) => {
        setTenants(data);
        setLoading(false);
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        console.error(''Error fetching tenants:'', error);
        setLoading(false);
      });
  }, []);

  if (loading) {
    return <div>Loading tenants...</div>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Active Tenants</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Slug</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {tenants.map((tenant) => (
            <tr key={tenant.id}>
              <td>{tenant.name}</td>
              <td>{tenant.slug}</td>
              <td>{tenant.status}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  );
}

export default ActiveTenants;
```

**Notes:**
- Replace `/api/tenants?status=active` with your actual API endpoint as needed.
- Each tenant object is expected to have at least: `id`, `name`, `slug`, and `status`.
- This is a simple table view; you can style it or adjust it to your needs.

